think The cluster-tree family has drilled very deep into graph theory (seams, subtree frames, MST leaves, centroid, radius, diameter, diameter path). Lindy-first crux-first, the strongest next move turns back to what a real surface DOES rather than one more tree invariant -- reconnecting to pixels and to a keeper's hand.
think Found a genuine still-open crux inside region_select itself: it reads the CROSSING set (any pixel enters), where the bounding box is a loose over-approximating prefilter. The dual question -- which objects a rectangle fully CONTAINS vs merely CLIPS -- is the window-versus-crossing selection every editor offers, and it carries the EXACT INVERSION of region_select's lesson: for containment the tight bounding box is not loose but EXACT, since the box is the object's own min/max pixel envelope, so all-pixels-inside iff box-inside.
think Designed image/region_partition.rye: contained_in_rect (boxes alone, no pixel scan), clipped_in_rect (crossing minus contained -- the crop-cut set, needing both map and boxes), partition_counts (the three class sizes summing to the component count), rect_contains_bbox (the exact test). Composes over region_select (crossing decision + QueryRect) and scene_manifest (per-object boxes) -- compose not braid, no new plane, a reading over a reading.
think Proved the inversion on metal: a full pixel scan (all_pixels_inside) agrees with the pure box test for every object and every rectangle -- no concavity gap, no hole gap, a holed ring contained by its box exactly. Three rectangles span the modes: contains-some (window keeps 2, crossing keeps 3, the clipped block the difference), contains-all, contains-none (clips three, the widest divergence), plus inclusive-corner containment, off-map all-OUTSIDE, BadRect refusals, determinism.
